(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["window",{"l:window/fixed-size-windows-one-in-one-out":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Adjacent windows of size k share k - 1 elements, so never recompute: add the element that enters, remove the one that leaves, and read the answer. Any aggregate with an inverse works this way (sum, count, letter counts, XOR). The first window costs O(k) and every slide O(1): O(n) in all instead of O(nk).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Invariant\u003c/b>: after processing index \u003ccode>r\u003c/code> (with \u003ccode>r &gt;= k - 1\u003c/code>), the state describes exactly \u003ccode>a[r - k + 1 .. r]\u003c/code>. Each step: add \u003ccode>a[r]\u003c/code>; if \u003ccode>r &gt;= k\u003c/code>, remove \u003ccode>a[r - k]\u003c/code>; then read.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Aggregates without an inverse\u003c/b>, such as maximum and minimum, cannot be \"un-added\". Pair the window with a monotonic deque (O(1) amortised, a later card) or a sorted container (O(log k)).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Matching a multiset\u003c/b> (is some window a rearrangement of \u003ccode>p\u003c/code>?). Comparing two \u003ccode>Counter\u003c/code>s per slide costs O(alphabet) and allocates. Instead keep \u003ccode>diff[c] = window count - p's count\u003c/code> for each letter and a number \u003ccode>bad\u003c/code> of letters with \u003ccode>diff != 0\u003c/code>. A slide changes two letters, so \u003ccode>bad\u003c/code> changes in O(1), and a match is \u003ccode>bad == 0\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Python specifics.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>sum(a[i:i + k])\u003c/code> inside a loop copies k references every time: O(nk). For a stream you cannot index back into, \u003ccode>collections.deque(maxlen=k)\u003c/code> keeps the last k items and drops the oldest automatically in O(1). Floating-point sums drift after millions of add-and-subtract steps; use integers (cents, milliseconds) or recompute the sum periodically.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Averages\u003c/b>: for a fixed k, maximise the sum and divide once at the end.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def window_sums(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Sum of every length-k window, left to right (empty if k &gt; len(a)).\"\"\"\n    if k &gt; len(a):\n        return []\n    s = sum(a[:k])\n    out = [s]\n    for r in range(k, len(a)):\n        s += a[r] - a[r - k]          # a[r] enters, a[r - k] leaves\n        out.append(s)\n    return out\n\n\ndef same_letter_windows(s, p):\n    \"\"\"Starts of the len(p)-long windows of s with exactly p's letter counts (letters a-z).\"\"\"\n    k = len(p)\n    if k == 0 or k &gt; len(s):\n        return []\n    diff = [0] * 26                   # window count minus p's count, per letter\n    for c in p:\n        diff[ord(c) - 97] -= 1\n    bad = sum(1 for d in diff if d)   # letters whose counts still differ\n    out = []\n\n    def bump(c, delta):\n        nonlocal bad\n        i = ord(c) - 97\n        bad -= diff[i] != 0\n        diff[i] += delta\n        bad += diff[i] != 0\n\n    for r, c in enumerate(s):\n        bump(c, +1)\n        if r &gt;= k:\n            bump(s[r - k], -1)\n        if r &gt;= k - 1 and bad == 0:\n            out.append(r - k + 1)\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Window sums of \u003ccode>[4, 2, 7, 1, 8, 3]\u003c/code> with k = 3; the highlighted cells are the current window.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First window a[0..2]: sum = 13, computed once in O(k)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index      0    1    2    3    4    5\na          \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    1    8    3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: 1 enters, 4 leaves: sum = 10 (best 13). One addition and one subtraction, whatever k is\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          4    \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>    8    3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: 8 enters, 2 leaves: sum = 16 (best 16). One addition and one subtraction, whatever k is\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          4    2    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: 3 enters, 7 leaves: sum = 12 (best 16). One addition and one subtraction, whatever k is\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          4    2    7    \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Off-by-one: the first full window ends at \u003ccode>r = k - 1\u003c/code>; record only from there. If \u003ccode>k &gt; n\u003c/code> there is no window at all.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>ord(c) - 97\u003c/code> assumes lowercase ASCII letters; for general text use a dict and the same \u003ccode>bad\u003c/code> counter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Rebuilding the window's \u003ccode>Counter\u003c/code> or slice on every step quietly turns O(n) into O(nk).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>same_letter_windows(\"cbabcacab\", \"abc\")\u003c/code> return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>[0, 2, 3, 6]\u003c/code>: the windows \"cba\", \"abc\", \"bca\" and \"cab\" each contain one a, one b and one c; \"bab\", \"cac\" and \"aca\" do not.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to slide a fixed window in O(1) per step and match windows against a multiset with a single counter.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:window/variable-windows-grow-shrink-and-why-it-works":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Advance the right edge one step at a time and add the new element to the window's state. If the window became invalid, advance the left edge until it is valid again, then record. Both edges only move forward, so the scan is O(n) amortised. It is correct only when validity is \u003cb>monotone\u003c/b>: shrinking a valid window keeps it valid (longest problems), or growing a valid window keeps it valid (shortest problems).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Invariant (longest)\u003c/b>: after the shrink loop, \u003ccode>[left, right]\u003c/code> is the longest valid window that ends at \u003ccode>right\u003c/code>. For each right end the best start is the smallest valid one, and with monotone validity that start never moves backwards as \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> grows, so \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> never has to.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> moves n times and \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> moves at most n times \u003ci>in total\u003c/i>, so the inner \u003ccode>while\u003c/code> does O(n) work over the whole run, not per step: O(n) amortised, times the cost of one state update.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Shortest variant\u003c/b> (sum at least \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, values non-negative): grow; while the window is valid, record its length and shrink. It needs the mirror condition: if \u003ccode>[l, r]\u003c/code> is valid, so is \u003ccode>[l, r + 1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Monotone validity, and the counterexample.\u003c/b> With negative numbers neither direction holds. Longest subarray with sum at most 0 in \u003ccode>[5, -6, 1]\u003c/code>: the window drops 5 (on its own it is invalid) and returns 2 for \u003ccode>[-6, 1]\u003c/code>, but the whole array sums to 0, length 3. A later negative value would have repaired the window that was thrown away. When values can be negative or the target is an exact sum, use prefix sums (with a hash map to count, or a sorted structure for inequalities): the next module.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>O(1) validity tests\u003c/b>: a running sum; a count map plus \"the key just added has count 2\"; \u003ccode>len(count)\u003c/code> distinct keys (delete zero counts); for character replacement, \u003ccode>length - max count &lt;= k\u003c/code>. That last one even works with \u003ccode>max count\u003c/code> kept as a running maximum that never decreases: a stale value can keep an invalid window alive, but only at a length some valid window already reached, so the recorded answer stays right.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Jumping instead of crawling\u003c/b>: for \"no repeats\", store each character's last index and set \u003ccode>left = max(left, last[c] + 1)\u003c/code>; same O(n), fewer steps.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_no_repeat(s):\n    \"\"\"Length of the longest substring with no repeated character (grow, then shrink).\"\"\"\n    count = defaultdict(int)\n    left = best = 0\n    for right, c in enumerate(s):\n        count[c] += 1                          # grow: c enters\n        while count[c] &gt; 1:                    # invalid: shrink until valid again\n            count[s[left]] -= 1\n            left += 1\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)     # [left, right] is the longest valid window ending at right\n    return best\n\n\ndef shortest_sum_at_least(a, target):\n    \"\"\"Length of the shortest subarray with sum &gt;= target (a &gt;= 0), or 0 if none.\"\"\"\n    total = left = 0\n    best = inf\n    for right, x in enumerate(a):\n        total += x                             # grow\n        while total &gt;= target:                 # valid: record, then try a shorter one\n            best = min(best, right - left + 1)\n            total -= a[left]\n            left += 1\n    return 0 if best == inf else best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Longest substring of \u003ccode>\"abcbad\"\u003c/code> with no repeated character: the count map and a valid or invalid marker after each move.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow over 'a', 'b', 'c' (r = 0, 1, 2), adding each to the count map. No count above 1: \u003cb>valid\u003c/b>, best = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   b   a   d\ncounts {a:1, b:1, c:1}   valid\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow: 'b' enters at r = 3. count['b'] = 2, so the window is \u003cb>invalid\u003c/b>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   a   d\ncounts {a:1, b:2, c:1}   INVALID\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Shrink from the left until valid: drop 'a' (left = 1), still two b's; drop 'b' (left = 2). Valid again; best stays 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         a   b   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   a   d\ncounts {b:1, c:1}   valid\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow: 'a' enters at r = 4. No count above 1: \u003cb>valid\u003c/b>, length 3, best = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         a   b   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   d\ncounts {a:1, b:1, c:1}   valid\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow: 'd' enters at r = 5. No count above 1: \u003cb>valid\u003c/b>, length 4, best = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         a   b   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>d\u003c/mark>\ncounts {a:1, b:1, c:1, d:1}   valid\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Recording inside the shrink loop for \"longest\" records invalid windows; recording after it for \"shortest\" misses the shorter valid ones.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Shrinking with \u003ccode>if\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>while\u003c/code>: one step may not restore validity.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With a \"sum at least target\" loop and \u003ccode>target &lt;= 0\u003c/code>, the loop can empty the window and push \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> past \u003ccode>right\u003c/code>; handle that case first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Applying the template to data with negative values. Before coding, check monotonicity with a brute force on small random inputs that include negatives.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>shortest_sum_at_least([1, 4, 2, 2, 5], 7)\u003c/code> return, and when is the answer first recorded?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>2. Lengths 3 are recorded at r = 2 ([1, 4, 2]), r = 3 ([4, 2, 2]) and r = 4 ([2, 2, 5]); at r = 4 the loop then drops the first 2, finds [2, 5] still sums to 7 and records 2.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know the grow-then-shrink template, why it is O(n) amortised, and how to check the monotone-validity condition that makes it correct.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:window/counting-windows-at-most-k-and-exactly-k":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>With monotone validity, the valid windows ending at \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> are exactly those starting anywhere in \u003ccode>[left, right]\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>right - left + 1\u003c/code> of them. Summing that over every right end counts all valid subarrays in O(n). \"Exactly K\" is not monotone, because shrinking can drop below K, but it is the difference of two monotone counts: exactly(K) = atMost(K) - atMost(K - 1).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why \u003ccode>right - left + 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/b> After the shrink loop \u003ccode>[left, right]\u003c/code> is valid, so every \u003ccode>[l, right]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>l &gt;= left\u003c/code> is valid too (it is a shrunk version), and every \u003ccode>l &lt; left\u003c/code> is invalid (that is why \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> moved past it).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Distinct counts.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>len(count)\u003c/code> is the number of distinct values only if keys are deleted when their count reaches 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why \"exactly K\" resists one window.\u003c/b> When the window holds K distinct values, several starts can work for the same end (dropping a duplicate keeps K), and one left pointer cannot represent that range. Two monotone counts can.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\"At least\" conditions\u003c/b> stay true as the window grows. Either use the complement (for distinct values, atLeast(K) = n(n + 1)/2 - atMost(K - 1)), or shrink while the window still qualifies and then add \u003ccode>left\u003c/code>: every start before \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> qualified with this end.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Products\u003c/b> (positive values): keep the running product, shrink while it is at least \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, and return 0 at once when \u003ccode>k &lt;= 1\u003c/code>, since no product of positive integers is below 1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost\u003c/b>: two O(n) passes for exactly-K; the count map holds at most K + 1 keys.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_at_most(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Number of subarrays with at most k distinct values.\"\"\"\n    if k &lt; 0:\n        return 0\n    count = defaultdict(int)\n    left = total = 0\n    for right, x in enumerate(a):\n        count[x] += 1\n        while len(count) &gt; k:\n            y = a[left]\n            count[y] -= 1\n            if count[y] == 0:\n                del count[y]                   # keep len(count) == number of distinct values\n            left += 1\n        total += right - left + 1              # every start in [left, right] works with this end\n    return total\n\n\ndef count_exactly(a, k):\n    return count_at_most(a, k) - count_at_most(a, k - 1)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>count_at_most([1, 2, 1, 3, 3], 2)\u003c/code>: each step adds the number of valid subarrays that end at \u003ccode>r\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 0: 1 enters, 1 distinct (valid). Subarrays ending here with a valid start: 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4\na         \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   2   1   3   3\ncounts {1:1}   + 1   total 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 1: 2 enters, 2 distinct (valid). Subarrays ending here with a valid start: 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4\na         \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   1   3   3\ncounts {1:1, 2:1}   + 2   total 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 2: 1 enters, 2 distinct (valid). Subarrays ending here with a valid start: 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4\na         \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   3   3\ncounts {1:2, 2:1}   + 3   total 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 3: 3 makes 3 distinct values, invalid. Shrink: drop 1 and 2 (left = 2). Now 2 subarrays end at r and start in [left, r]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4\na         1   2   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   3\ncounts {1:1, 3:1}   + 2   total 8\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 4: 3 enters, 2 distinct (valid). Subarrays ending here with a valid start: 3. atMost(2) = 11; atMost(1) = 6, so exactly 2 distinct: 11 - 6 = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4\na         1   2   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\ncounts {1:1, 3:2}   + 3   total 11\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keeping zero counts in the dict, so \u003ccode>len(count)\u003c/code> overstates the number of distinct values and the window shrinks too far.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Adding 1 per valid window instead of \u003ccode>right - left + 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>atMost(K - 1)\u003c/code> with K = 0: return 0 for a negative limit rather than running the loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In the product version without the \u003ccode>k &lt;= 1\u003c/code> check, the shrink loop runs \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> past \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> and divides by elements that are no longer in the window.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which subarrays make up \u003ccode>count_exactly([1, 2, 1, 3, 3], 2) = 5\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>[1, 2], [2, 1], [1, 2, 1], [1, 3] and [1, 3, 3]. atMost(2) = 11 counts these plus the six single-value subarrays ([1], [2], [1], [3], [3], [3, 3]), which atMost(1) = 6 removes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to count valid subarrays in O(n) with right - left + 1, and how to turn a non-monotone \"exactly\" into two monotone \"at most\" counts.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:window/minimum-covering-window-the-formed-counter":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Grow the right edge until the window covers every requirement, then shrink from the left while it still does, recording each covering window. To test \"covers\" in O(1), keep \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code>: the number of distinct required characters whose count in the window has reached its requirement. It changes only when a count crosses its threshold, upwards while growing and downwards while shrinking.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Monotone in the \"shortest\" direction\u003c/b>: if \u003ccode>[l, r]\u003c/code> covers \u003ccode>t\u003c/code>, every larger window does too. So this is the shortest-window template with a cheaper validity test.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Bookkeeping\u003c/b>: when \u003ccode>have[c]\u003c/code> becomes exactly \u003ccode>need[c]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>formed += 1\u003c/code> (a surplus copy does not count again). When a drop makes \u003ccode>have[d] &lt; need[d]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>formed -= 1\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>formed == required\u003c/code> exactly when the window covers \u003ccode>t\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost\u003c/b>: each edge moves at most m = len(s) times with O(1) work per move, plus O(n) to count \u003ccode>t\u003c/code>: O(m + n) time and O(alphabet) memory. Comparing two Counters at each step would cost O(alphabet) per step instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Record positions, not slices\u003c/b>: store \u003ccode>(length, start)\u003c/code> and slice once at the end; slicing on every improvement copies characters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Relatives\u003c/b>: the fixed-window \u003ccode>bad\u003c/code> counter for anagram windows is the same idea; \"substrings containing all three letters\" counts with the \"at least\" trick; windows measured in whole words step through the text one word length at a time.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def min_window(s, t):\n    \"\"\"Shortest substring of s containing every character of t with multiplicity, or ''.\"\"\"\n    need = Counter(t)\n    required = len(need)                       # distinct characters to satisfy\n    have = defaultdict(int)\n    formed = 0                                 # how many of them are satisfied now\n    best_len, best_start = inf, 0\n    left = 0\n    for right, c in enumerate(s):\n        have[c] += 1\n        if c in need and have[c] == need[c]:\n            formed += 1                        # c just became satisfied\n        while formed == required:\n            if right - left + 1 &lt; best_len:\n                best_len, best_start = right - left + 1, left\n            d = s[left]\n            have[d] -= 1\n            if d in need and have[d] &lt; need[d]:\n                formed -= 1                    # d just became unsatisfied\n            left += 1\n    return \"\" if best_len == inf else s[best_start:best_start + best_len]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>min_window(\"xcbcab\", \"abc\")\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>have\u003c/code> shows the required characters only, and the highlighted cells are the window.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow over x, c, b, c. The second c pushes have[c] to 2, more than needed; formed counts only characters whose need is met: c and b, so 2 of 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         \u003cmark>x\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   a   b\nhave {a:0, b:1, c:2}   formed 2/3   best -\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow: a arrives, have[a] reaches need[a], so formed = 3/3: the window covers t. Now shrink while it still does\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         \u003cmark>x\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   b\nhave {a:1, b:1, c:2}   formed 3/3   best -\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Record best = 'xcbca' (length 5). Drop 'x': not in t, formed stays 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         x   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   b\nhave {a:1, b:1, c:2}   formed 3/3   best xcbca\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Record best = 'cbca'. Drop 'c': have[c] falls 2 -&gt; 1, still &gt;= need, so formed stays 3 and shrinking continues\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         x   c   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   b\nhave {a:1, b:1, c:1}   formed 3/3   best cbca\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Record best = 'bca'. Drop 'b': have[b] falls below need, formed = 2. The window is no longer covering: stop shrinking, grow again\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         x   c   b   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   b\nhave {a:1, b:0, c:1}   formed 2/3   best bca\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow: b arrives, formed = 3 again for 'cab' (length 3, not shorter than 'bca', so best stays). Drop 'c': formed = 2. The scan ends: the answer is 'bca'\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         x   c   b   c   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>\nhave {a:1, b:1, c:0}   formed 2/3   best bca\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Incrementing \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code> on every copy of a required character: with \u003ccode>t = \"aa\"\u003c/code>, a single \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> must not complete the requirement.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Testing \u003ccode>have[d] &lt;= need[d]\u003c/code> after the decrement instead of \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code> then drops while the window still covers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Using a \u003ccode>defaultdict\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>need\u003c/code> and reading \u003ccode>need[c]\u003c/code> for characters outside \u003ccode>t\u003c/code> inserts them, which breaks \u003ccode>len(need)\u003c/code> if it is computed later; test \u003ccode>c in need\u003c/code> first, as the template does.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty \u003ccode>t\u003c/code>, or a \u003ccode>t\u003c/code> longer than \u003ccode>s\u003c/code>: return \"\" without scanning.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>min_window(\"abxaab\", \"aab\")\u003c/code> return, and when does \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code> first reach 2?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\"aab\". formed reaches 2 at r = 3, when the window \"abxa\" first holds two a's and a b. Later windows of length 4 tie, and at r = 5 dropping the x leaves \"aab\", length 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to find minimum covering windows in O(m + n) with a satisfied-requirements counter.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:window/window-extremes-and-when-to-leave-the-window-beh":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A window needs an aggregate you can update when an element leaves and a validity rule that is monotone. Maximum and minimum cannot be \"un-added\", so pair the window with a \u003cb>monotonic deque\u003c/b> of indices (O(1) amortised) or a heap with lazy deletion (O(log n)). If validity is not monotone, the window is the wrong tool: switch to prefix sums, sort first, or fix one more parameter so that each pass becomes monotone.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deque invariant\u003c/b>: indices increase from front to back and their values strictly decrease, so the front is the maximum of the current window. A new value first pops every smaller or equal value from the back (they are older and smaller, so they can never be a maximum again), then the front is dropped if it has left the window. Each index is pushed once and popped at most once: O(n) total. The monotonic stacks module proves this in depth and reuses it for next-greater problems.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Lazy heap\u003c/b>: push \u003ccode>(-value, index)\u003c/code>; before reading, pop tops whose index has left the window. Stale entries can pile up inside the heap, so it may hold up to n items: O(n log n), simpler to adapt when the window moves irregularly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Python\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code> appends and pops at both ends in O(1) (it is a linked list of 64-slot blocks); \u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code> shifts everything, O(n).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>When the plain window breaks, match the symptom:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Symptom\u003c/th>\u003cth>Repair\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Need the max or min of every window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>monotonic deque of indices\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Values can be negative, or the target is an exact sum\u003c/td>\u003ctd>prefix sums plus a hash map of earlier prefixes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Elements can be taken only from both ends (reduce x to zero)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>complement: the kept middle is one window with sum total - x\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The condition is about values, not positions (make k increments)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sort, then window over the sorted values\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Adding a character can help or hurt (every letter at least k times)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>fix the number of distinct letters u = 1..26; each pass is an at-most-u window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(26n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def window_max(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Maximum of every length-k window; deque holds indices with decreasing values.\"\"\"\n    dq, out = deque(), []\n    for r, x in enumerate(a):\n        while dq and a[dq[-1]] &lt;= x:\n            dq.pop()                           # smaller, older values can never be a maximum again\n        dq.append(r)\n        if dq[0] &lt;= r - k:\n            dq.popleft()                       # the front left the window\n        if r &gt;= k - 1:\n            out.append(a[dq[0]])\n    return out\n\n\ndef window_max_heap(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Same answers with a lazy-deletion heap: O(n log n).\"\"\"\n    heap, out = [], []\n    for r, x in enumerate(a):\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-x, r))\n        while heap[0][1] &lt;= r - k:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)                # stale: its index left the window\n        if r &gt;= k - 1:\n            out.append(-heap[0][0])\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maximum of every window of size 3 in \u003ccode>[5, 3, 4, 1, 2, 0]\u003c/code>; the deque line shows \u003ccode>index(value)\u003c/code> from front to back.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 0: 5 enters\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   3   4   1   2   0\ndeque  0(5)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 1: 3 enters\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   4   1   2   0\ndeque  0(5) 1(3)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 2: 4 enters; pop 3 from the back (smaller and older: never a maximum again); max = a[0] = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   1   2   0\ndeque  0(5) 2(4)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 3: 1 enters; front index 0 left the window: popleft; max = a[2] = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         5   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   2   0\ndeque  2(4) 3(1)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 4: 2 enters; pop 1 from the back (smaller and older: never a maximum again); max = a[2] = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         5   3   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   0\ndeque  2(4) 4(2)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 5: 0 enters; front index 2 left the window: popleft; max = a[4] = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         5   3   4   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\ndeque  4(2) 5(0)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Storing values instead of indices in the deque: you can no longer tell when the front has left the window.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Expiring the front with \u003ccode>if\u003c/code> works for fixed windows (at most one index leaves per step); a variable window whose left edge can jump needs \u003ccode>while\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Popping with \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> keeps equal values in the deque; still correct, just longer. Popping from the front when you meant the back breaks the invariant.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Forcing a window onto data with negative values: see the counterexample in the variable-window card.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which tool would you use for \"count subarrays whose sum is exactly k\" when values may be negative, and why not a window?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prefix sums with a hash map of how often each earlier prefix sum occurred. With negative values, shrinking a window can raise or lower its sum, so there is no left edge that only moves forward; validity is not monotone.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>window_max([5, 3, 4, 1, 2, 0], 3)\u003c/code> return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>[5, 4, 4, 2]\u003c/code>, as in the trace: 5 leaves at r = 3, 4 leaves at r = 5.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to track window maxima in O(n) and how to spot and repair the cases where a plain sliding window does not apply.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:window/rolling-metrics-over-a-log-stream":"\u003cp>Windows over time are what monitoring and rate limiting compute all day. Prometheus' \u003ccode>rate(x[5m])\u003c/code> aggregates each series over the trailing five minutes; a rate limiter asks \"how many requests has this client sent in the last minute?\" on every request; Flink and Kafka Streams run the same windows over event streams. Here you compute these metrics over a synthetic day of one million requests, check them against a naive scan, and measure the two shortcuts real limiters take: fixed clock-minute buckets and Cloudflare's two-counter estimate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>rolling_lab.py\u003c/code>. The time windows are half-open, \u003ccode>(t - width, t]\u003c/code>, and the log is sorted by time, so the left edge only moves forward.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\"\"\"The rolling-metrics lab from dsa/09-window.md, as printed there (run: python3 rolling_lab.py).\"\"\"\nimport random\nimport time\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\n\ndef make_log(n, seed=0):\n    \"\"\"n (timestamp, user) requests over one day, sorted by time, with a busy lunchtime and a few heavy users.\"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    times = sorted(rng.uniform(0, 86_400) if rng.random() &lt; 0.8 else rng.gauss(45_000, 1_800) % 86_400\n                   for _ in range(n))\n    users = [rng.randrange(20) if rng.random() &lt; 0.3 else rng.randrange(100_000) for _ in range(n)]\n    return times, users\n\n\ndef last_minute_naive(times):\n    \"\"\"For each request i: how many requests fell in (t_i - 60, t_i]. Scans backwards: O(n * w).\"\"\"\n    out = []\n    for i, t in enumerate(times):\n        j = i\n        while j &gt;= 0 and times[j] &gt; t - 60:\n            j -= 1\n        out.append(i - j)\n    return out\n\n\ndef last_minute(times, width=60):\n    \"\"\"Same answers with a variable window: the left edge only moves forward. O(n).\"\"\"\n    out, left = [], 0\n    for right, t in enumerate(times):\n        while times[left] &lt;= t - width:\n            left += 1\n        out.append(right - left + 1)\n    return out\n\n\ndef distinct_users_naive(times, users, width=300):\n    out = []\n    for i, t in enumerate(times):\n        seen, j = set(), i\n        while j &gt;= 0 and times[j] &gt; t - width:\n            seen.add(users[j])\n            j -= 1\n        out.append(len(seen))\n    return out\n\n\ndef distinct_users(times, users, width=300):\n    \"\"\"Distinct users in (t - width, t] at each request: window plus a count map.\"\"\"\n    count = defaultdict(int)\n    out, left = [], 0\n    for right, t in enumerate(times):\n        count[users[right]] += 1\n        while times[left] &lt;= t - width:\n            u = users[left]\n            count[u] -= 1\n            if count[u] == 0:\n                del count[u]\n            left += 1\n        out.append(len(count))\n    return out\n\n\ndef fixed_minute_max(times):\n    \"\"\"Busiest clock minute ([60k, 60k + 60)): what a fixed-window counter sees.\"\"\"\n    buckets = defaultdict(int)\n    for t in times:\n        buckets[int(t // 60)] += 1\n    return max(buckets.values())\n\n\ndef sliding_counter_estimate(times, width=60):\n    \"\"\"Cloudflare-style estimate at each request: previous bucket weighted by overlap plus current bucket.\"\"\"\n    buckets = defaultdict(int)\n    out = []\n    for t in times:\n        b = int(t // width)\n        buckets[b] += 1\n        elapsed = t - b * width\n        out.append(buckets[b - 1] * (width - elapsed) / width + buckets[b])\n    return out\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    times, users = make_log(1_000_000)\n    small = times[:10_000]\n    t0 = time.perf_counter(); a = last_minute_naive(small); t1 = time.perf_counter()\n    b = last_minute(small); t2 = time.perf_counter()\n    assert a == b\n    print(f\"last minute, 10k requests: naive {t1 - t0:.2f} s, window {t2 - t1:.4f} s\")\n    t0 = time.perf_counter(); a = distinct_users_naive(small, users[:10_000]); t1 = time.perf_counter()\n    b = distinct_users(small, users[:10_000]); t2 = time.perf_counter()\n    assert a == b\n    print(f\"distinct users in 5 min, 10k requests: naive {t1 - t0:.2f} s, window {t2 - t1:.4f} s\")\n\n    t0 = time.perf_counter(); rate = last_minute(times); t1 = time.perf_counter()\n    dist = distinct_users(times, users); t2 = time.perf_counter()\n    print(f\"1M requests: last-minute counts {t1 - t0:.2f} s, distinct users {t2 - t1:.2f} s\")\n    busiest = max(range(len(times)), key=rate.__getitem__)\n    print(f\"busiest sliding minute: {rate[busiest]} requests, ending at t = {times[busiest]:.0f} s\")\n    print(f\"busiest clock minute (fixed window): {fixed_minute_max(times)} requests\")\n    print(f\"most distinct users in 5 min: {max(dist)}\")\n    est = sliding_counter_estimate(times)\n    err = [abs(e - r) / r for e, r in zip(est, rate)]\n    print(f\"sliding-counter estimate: mean relative error {sum(err) / len(err):.2%}, max {max(err):.1%}\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Before running it, predict: how much faster is the window than the naive scan for 10,000 requests, given that a minute holds a few hundred requests and five minutes a few thousand? Will the busiest clock minute be higher or lower than the busiest sliding minute?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>python3 rolling_lab.py\u003c/code> (about 20 seconds; the naive distinct-users scan is the slow part). The asserts prove the window versions agree with the naive ones.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Explain the fixed-window result: why can the busiest clock minute never exceed the busiest sliding minute, and why does a limit enforced on clock minutes let up to twice the limit through in 60 seconds (L requests at 0:59, L more at 1:00)?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Look at the two-counter estimate's error. Sort the requests by error and look at the timestamps of the worst ones, then compare the mean error during the lunchtime peak (40,000 &lt; t &lt; 50,000) with the rest of the day. Which is larger, and why?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Turn \u003ccode>last_minute\u003c/code> into a limiter: a class with \u003ccode>allow(t)\u003c/code> that keeps a \u003ccode>deque\u003c/code> of accepted timestamps for one client, pops from the left while the oldest is at least 60 seconds old, and accepts only if fewer than L remain. Test it with a burst of 2L requests around a minute boundary and check that it never accepts more than L in any 60-second span.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For 10,000 requests the naive last-minute count takes about a second and the window a few milliseconds, a speed-up of over 100x; for distinct users the naive scan takes several seconds against tens of milliseconds. The gap grows with the number of requests per window, because the naive scan rereads the whole window for every request.\u003c/li>\u003cli>On a million requests both window passes finish in one or two seconds of pure Python.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With the seed in the script: busiest sliding minute 3,387 requests (during the lunchtime peak, around t = 44,800 s), busiest clock minute 3,288. A clock minute is one particular 60-second window, so it can only miss the peak, never exceed it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The two-counter estimate has a mean relative error of about 1% and a worst case of about 10%. The estimate assumes the previous minute's requests were spread evenly, and random clumps break that assumption; the worst cases sit off-peak, and the mean error is about 0.8% during the peak against 1.3% elsewhere, because relative noise shrinks as counts grow. Cloudflare measured something different, wrong allow or block decisions, and found them on 0.003% of 400 million requests: a limiter only needs the estimate to be accurate near the limit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The sliding-log limiter from step 6 is exact but stores one timestamp per accepted request per client; the two-counter version stores two integers. Cloudflare chose the estimate for this kind of reason: a per-request log was too costly in memory and processing, while two counters need only the GET, SET and INCR operations their memcached cluster offered atomically.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>","p:643":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The best average over contiguous blocks of exactly k elements.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two neighbouring blocks share all but two elements. What changes when the block moves by one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Fixed-size window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep the block sum: add the element entering on the right and subtract the one leaving on the left. Track the best sum and divide by k once at the end.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:209":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The shortest contiguous run whose sum reaches a target, and every value is positive.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With positive values, what happens to the sum when you drop the leftmost element of a run that already reaches the target?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Extend right; while the sum is still at least the target, record the length and drop from the left. Positivity makes validity monotone, so the left edge never moves back and the scan is O(n).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:3":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest substring with no character repeated.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Grow a run to the right. When a repeat appears, what is the least you must cut from the left?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep counts (or the last index) of each character in the window; on a repeat, move left past the earlier copy, never backwards (left = max(left, last[c] + 1)). Record the length after each repair.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(alphabet) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:567":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Does any substring of s2 contain exactly the letters of s1, in any order?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any such substring has exactly len(s1) characters. How can you compare letter counts without recounting every candidate?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Fixed-size window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Slide a window of len(s1) over s2 with 26 count differences and a counter of letters whose difference is non-zero; each slide changes two letters, and a match is a zero counter.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(26) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:424":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest substring that becomes a single repeated letter after at most k replacements.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For a given substring, how many replacements are needed? It depends on one count only.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window\u003c/p>\u003cp>A window needs length - (count of its most common letter) replacements; shrink while that exceeds k. The maximum count can even be kept as a running maximum that never decreases: a stale value never lets the window exceed a length that some valid window reached.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(26) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:713":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count contiguous subarrays whose product is below k, with positive values.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For a fixed right end, which left ends work? Are they scattered or one block?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> At most K, exactly K\u003c/p>\u003cp>Shrink while the product is at least k; then every start in [left, right] works, so add right - left + 1. Return 0 straight away when k &lt;= 1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:904":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest run that uses at most two different kinds of item.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Rephrase it as a window condition on the number of distinct values. What do you do when a third kind enters?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> At most K, exactly K\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a count per kind in the window and delete kinds whose count reaches zero; shrink from the left while there are more than two kinds, and record the length after each step.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space (at most three keys)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1658":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Remove elements only from the two ends so that the removed ones sum to x, with as few removals as possible.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Look at what is left over rather than what is removed. What shape does it have?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Removing from both ends leaves one contiguous middle block, which must sum to total - x. Values are positive, so find the longest window with exactly that sum (shrink while the sum is too big); the answer is n minus its length, or -1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:76":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The shortest substring of s that contains every character of t, counting repeats.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Grow until the window covers t, then shrink while it still does. How can you tell \"covers\" in O(1)?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Minimum covering window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep need counts, window counts and formed, the number of distinct characters whose need is met; it changes only when a count crosses its threshold. Record while formed equals the number of distinct characters in t.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m + n) time, O(alphabet) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:992":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count subarrays with exactly K distinct integers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Shrinking a window with exactly K distinct values can keep it at K, so one left pointer cannot capture all valid starts. Which related count is monotone?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> At most K, exactly K\u003c/p>\u003cp>Exactly K = atMost(K) - atMost(K - 1), where atMost adds right - left + 1 for each right end of a standard shrinking window over distinct counts.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1838":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Up to k increments of +1; maximise how often one value appears.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Only raise values towards a value that already exists, and the nearest smaller values are the cheapest to raise. What order makes \"nearest\" contiguous?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort. Window [l, r] can be levelled up to a[r] when a[r] * (r - l + 1) - window sum &lt;= k; this cost only grows as the window grows, so shrink while it exceeds k and keep the longest window.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:395":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest substring in which every character appears at least k times.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Adding a character can make the window better or worse, so no shrink rule is monotone. Which extra quantity, if you fixed it, would make one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> At most K, exactly K\u003c/p>\u003cp>For each u = 1..26, run a window that allows at most u distinct letters, tracking how many letters reach k; whenever distinct == u and all u reach k, the window qualifies. Divide and conquer on letters rarer than k also works.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(26 n) time, O(26) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>"}]);
